_Last checked: 24 November 2026. Every figure below was read from the publication it links to. These are survey and vendor-test results, not counts of all developers._

## At a glance

1. **Most developers use AI tools.** 84% of Stack Overflow's 2025 respondents used or planned to use them (n = 33,662, [Stack Overflow](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/ai)). Google's DORA report put use at work at 90% for June and July 2025 (nearly 5,000 respondents, [DORA](https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/2025_state_of_ai_assisted_software_development.pdf)).
2. **Few say they vibe code.** 72.2% said it is not part of their professional work, and 11.9% said yes (Stack Overflow 2025, n = 26,564).
3. **Trust trails use.** 46% distrusted the accuracy of AI tools and 33% trusted it (Stack Overflow 2025, n = 33,244).
4. **Vendor tests of raw models show security gaps.** Veracode found a 56% average security pass rate in July 2026, for raw models with no security prompting ([Veracode](https://www.veracode.com/news/llms-are-getting-smarter-but-not-safer-veracode-2026-genai-code-security-report-finds-ai-generated-code-security-has-stalled-at-56%25-pass-rate/)).
5. **GitHub reports 180 million-plus developers.** About 80% of new ones used Copilot in their first week ([Octoverse 2025](https://github.blog/news-insights/octoverse/octoverse-a-new-developer-joins-github-every-second-as-ai-leads-typescript-to-1/)).

Each figure comes from a different group of people in a different period. I list the base next to every number. For AI search usage rather than developer tools, see my [AI search statistics](/blog/ai-search-statistics/).

## Which figure can I cite?

Use the row that matches your claim. Quote its base and date in the same sentence.

