## How many developers use AI tools?

Between 80% and 90% in the recent surveys. The wording of each question differs. I do not join them into one trend.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Five surveys, five questions" title="Headline AI use among developers, by survey and measure" data="Stack Overflow 2025 use or plan to use:84|DORA 2025 use AI at work:90|JetBrains 2025 regular AI tool use:85|Stack Overflow 2026 an hour or more a day:80|JetBrains 2026 agents weekly at work:90" suffix="%" source="Stack Overflow 2025 (n = 33,662) and 2026 (n = 10,926); DORA 2025 (nearly 5,000); JetBrains 2025 (24,534) and 2026 (over 15,000 professional developers)" /%}

- **Stack Overflow 2025.** 84% of respondents used or planned to use AI tools. 51% of professional developers used them daily (n = 26,004) ([survey](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/ai)).
- **Stack Overflow 2026.** The survey drew 30,903 responses from 169 countries. Of the 10,926 who answered the daily-use chart, 30.9% used AI for four hours or more a day. The three bands of an hour or more add to 80.3% ([survey](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2026/ai)).
- **DORA 2025.** 90% of respondents used AI as part of their work ([report](https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/2025_state_of_ai_assisted_software_development.pdf)). The survey ran from 13 June to 21 July 2025.
- **JetBrains 2025.** 85% regularly used AI tools for coding and development. 62% relied on at least one AI coding assistant, agent or code editor. The base was 24,534 developers in 194 countries ([post of 15 October 2025](https://blog.jetbrains.com/research/2025/10/state-of-developer-ecosystem-2025/)).
- **JetBrains 2026.** 90% of professional developers used AI coding agents at work at least weekly and 68% daily in May to July 2026. The survey was reweighted to represent the global developer population ([post of 18 August 2026](https://blog.jetbrains.com/research/2026/08/ai-coding-agent-adoption-2026/)).

## How many developers are vibe coding?

A minority. The term comes from a post by Andrej Karpathy on [X](https://x.com/karpathy/status/1886192184808149383) on 2 February 2025. He wrote of "a new kind of coding I call 'vibe coding', where you fully give in to the vibes, embrace exponentials, and forget that the code even exists."

The Stack Overflow 2025 survey asked whether vibe coding is part of professional development work. It defined the term as the process of generating software from LLM prompts. 26,564 respondents answered ([survey](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/ai)).

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2025" title="Is vibe coding part of your professional development work?" data="No:72.2|No, emphatically:5.3|Yes:11.9|Yes, somewhat:2.8|I have tried it:2.1|Yes, emphatically:0.4" suffix="%" highlight="No" source="Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2025, AI section, n = 26,564. Not sure (1.2%) and uncategorised (4%) are left out." /%}

The three yes answers add up to 15.1% (11.9% plus 2.8% plus 0.4%). That sum is mine. The survey page prints the three shares and not the total. The 2026 survey page I read has no vibe coding question, so 2025 is the newest edition with one.

JetBrains measured something nearby for 2026. It asked over 15,000 professional developers what share of last month's work code was fully generated by AI agents. The average answer was about 47%. About 22% relied almost entirely on agents, with more than 80% of their code agent-generated ([post of 26 August 2026](https://blog.jetbrains.com/research/2026/08/how-much-code-do-developers-really-let-agents-write/)). That is agent use, not vibe coding as Stack Overflow defined it.

## Do developers trust AI output?

Not fully. Three surveys give three different pictures because they asked different questions.

| Survey | Question | Result | Base |
|---|---|---|---|
| [Stack Overflow 2025](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/ai) | Trust in the accuracy of AI tool output | 33% trust (3.1% highly), 46% distrust (19.6% highly) | 33,244 |
| [Stack Overflow 2026](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2026/) | When do you trust AI output? | 48% when they can verify it, 12% do not trust it for most tasks | 14,304 |
| [DORA 2025](https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/2025_state_of_ai_assisted_software_development.pdf) | Trust in the quality of AI-generated output | 24% a great deal or a lot, 30% a little or none | Nearly 5,000 |

The Stack Overflow 2026 home page also says more than 87% of respondents had some level of trust in AI's outputs, up from 31% last year. The 2025 page reports 33% trust. The 2026 chart uses a different question. I cannot reconcile the two, so I do not compare them.

Two more 2025 Stack Overflow figures show the friction (n = 31,476). 66% named "AI solutions that are almost right, but not quite" as a problem. 45.2% said debugging AI-generated code is more time-consuming. Separately, 87% of 28,930 respondents agreed they were concerned about the accuracy of AI agents. 81% had concerns about security and privacy of data.

## How secure is AI-generated code?

It depends on what is tested. The only figures I use here come from Veracode. It is a security vendor. It designed the 2025 tests and scored them with its own static analysis tool.

{% stats %}
{% stat value="56%" label="average security pass rate, 11 raw models, 2026" source="Veracode, 28 July 2026" /%}
{% stat value="45%" label="of 80 test cases had an OWASP Top 10 flaw, 2025" source="Veracode, 30 July 2025" /%}
{% /stats %}

**The 2026 report** (28 July 2026) found an average pass rate of 56%, "virtually unchanged" from the year before. The models failed nearly 44% of the time with no security prompting. The top model scored 68% and the lowest 50%. By language, Python scored 63% and Java 30%.

**The 2025 report** (30 July 2025) used 80 curated code-completion tasks across more than 100 models. In 45% of test cases the models introduced a flaw in the OWASP Top 10. Java failed over 70% of the time. Python, C# and JavaScript failed between 38% and 45%. Cross-site scripting (CWE-80) failed 86% and log injection (CWE-117) 88%.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Veracode 2026 GenAI Code Security Report" title="Security pass rate of raw models, 2026" data="Top model:68|Average:56|Lowest model:50" suffix="%" highlight="Average" source="Veracode, press release of 28 July 2026. 11 models on the leaderboard." /%}

Veracode states the limit itself. The tests ran against raw models and not agents or production environments with tooling, guardrails or human review. So these results do not say how code that went through review or scanning performs. They also do not say that AI-generated code is insecure in general. Veracode's own advice is to treat AI code like any unreviewed code.

The two reports are not one series. The 2025 figure counts test cases with a flaw. The 2026 figure is a pass rate across a different set of models. I do not subtract one from the other.

## How big is AI-assisted development on GitHub?

GitHub's Octoverse covers its own platform from 1 September 2024 to 31 August 2025. It is a vendor report and a platform count, not a sample of all developers.

{% stats %}
{% stat value="180M+" label="developers on GitHub" source="GitHub Octoverse 2025" /%}
{% stat value="36M+" label="developers joined in one year" source="GitHub Octoverse 2025" /%}
{% stat value="80%" label="of new developers used Copilot in their first week" source="GitHub Octoverse 2025" /%}
{% /stats %}

GitHub also reported a record 518.7 million pull requests merged (+29% on the year before) and more than 986 million commits in 2025 (+25%). AI-related repositories passed 4.3 million ([Octoverse 2025](https://github.blog/news-insights/octoverse/octoverse-a-new-developer-joins-github-every-second-as-ai-leads-typescript-to-1/)). The post gives the Copilot share as "80%" in one place and "nearly 80%" in another. I write about 80%.

## What this means for a site built this way

These sources measure how developers work. None of them measures how sites built with AI tools perform in search or AI answers. I do not draw a conclusion about rankings from them.

What a site built this way needs is the same as any other site. The content has to reach crawlers in the HTML they receive. My [JavaScript SEO guide for developers](/blog/javascript-seo-for-developers/) covers how to test that. My [AI visibility](/ai-visibility/) page explains how I check whether a site is cited in AI answers.

## Methodology and sources

| Source | Publisher | Published | Sample and period | Type |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| [Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2025, AI section](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/ai) | Stack Overflow | 2025 (no date on page) | Each question has its own n, from 26,004 to 33,662 on this page. The page gives no total or fieldwork dates | Online survey |
| [Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2026](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2026/) | Stack Overflow | 2026 (no date on page) | 30,903 responses, 169 countries. Each chart has its own n | Online survey |
| [2025 DORA State of AI-assisted Software Development](https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/2025_state_of_ai_assisted_software_development.pdf) | Google Cloud (DORA) | 23 September 2025 announcement | Nearly 5,000 technology professionals, 13 June to 21 July 2025 | Survey and interviews |
| [State of Developer Ecosystem 2025](https://blog.jetbrains.com/research/2025/10/state-of-developer-ecosystem-2025/) | JetBrains Research | 15 October 2025 | 24,534 developers, 194 countries | Vendor survey |
| [AI coding agent adoption](https://blog.jetbrains.com/research/2026/08/ai-coding-agent-adoption-2026/) and [share of code](https://blog.jetbrains.com/research/2026/08/how-much-code-do-developers-really-let-agents-write/) | JetBrains Research | 18 and 26 August 2026 | Over 15,000 professional developers, May to July 2026, reweighted | Vendor survey |
| [2026 GenAI Code Security Report](https://www.veracode.com/news/llms-are-getting-smarter-but-not-safer-veracode-2026-genai-code-security-report-finds-ai-generated-code-security-has-stalled-at-56%25-pass-rate/) | Veracode | 28 July 2026 | 11 raw models on the leaderboard. Task count not stated | Vendor benchmark |
| [2025 GenAI Code Security Report](https://www.veracode.com/press-release/ai-generated-code-poses-major-security-risks-in-nearly-half-of-all-development-tasks-veracode-research-reveals/) | Veracode | 30 July 2025 | 80 tasks, more than 100 models | Vendor benchmark |
| [Octoverse 2025](https://github.blog/news-insights/octoverse/octoverse-a-new-developer-joins-github-every-second-as-ai-leads-typescript-to-1/) | GitHub | 28 October 2025, updated 28 February 2026 | GitHub platform data, 1 September 2024 to 31 August 2025 | Vendor platform data |

Limits that apply to every figure on this page:

- Survey results describe the people who answered. The Stack Overflow pages I read do not describe a sampling method.
- JetBrains, Veracode and GitHub sell developer tools or security products. Their reports are vendor research.
- The surveys define AI use differently. Do not put two of them in one comparison.
- I did not use figures from blogs that repeat these reports. A figure is on this page only if I read it at its publisher.
- Stack Overflow's 2025 and 2026 pages give no publication date and no overall respondent total for 2025. I state neither.

## How to cite this page

Cite the primary source for any figure you publish. A safe pattern:

> In the Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2025, 72.2% of the 26,564 respondents who answered said vibe coding is not part of their professional development work.

To cite this summary: Sunny Patel, "Vibe coding statistics: usage, trust and security", sunnypatel.co.uk/blog/vibe-coding-statistics/, last checked 24 November 2026.
